## Notes for the reviewer

Expected RED reasons before the Implementer (the suite was authored but not executed by the writer, which has no run tool; the dynamic RED check is test-reviewer's):
- test/life-areas.test.ts: the module src/shared/life-areas.ts does not resolve (plan Task 1).
- test/life-areas-routes.test.ts and test/life-areas-export.test.ts: /api/life-areas is not mounted, so every request answers 404 (plan Tasks 5 and 6). AC-7 carries a positive control (the same request with the right token must reach the route) so the existing 401 of the global gate cannot make it pass alone.
- test/life-areas-task-routes.test.ts: an unknown lifeAreaId is not validated on POST /api/tasks, and the list ignores lifeAreaId, answering every Task and answering 200 for an empty lifeAreaId= (plan Tasks 7 and 8).
- test/life-areas-series.test.ts: an unknown or empty lifeAreaId on POST and PATCH /api/series is not validated (plan Tasks 4 and 9).
- test/task-update.test.ts: lifeAreaId is still refused as "not editable", so set and clear answer 400 and the refusal of an unknown id carries the wrong reason (asserted not to match /editable/).

Cases expected GREEN before the Implementer, on purpose, because the behaviour already exists in code and no test covered it: Task create with an existing area and with the key absent or null; series create stamping the series and its first occurrence and the successor copying the area (src/worker/successor.ts:74); series PATCH moving the series and its open non-detached occurrence only, leaving a detached occurrence and the closed rows alone, and clearing with null; the omitted-key case on Task PATCH; a non-string lifeAreaId on series PATCH. They pin the paths the new existence check sits on. The AC-7 refusals are green on their own for the same reason test/reminders.test.ts documents; each is paired with a control that is red now.

Names taken from the plan rather than the PRD (PRD AC-12 left them open): LIFE_AREA_NAME_MAX, LIFE_AREA_LIMIT, validateLifeAreaName returning "blank" or "too_long" or null, lifeAreaNameKey, compareLifeAreaNames, the list body { lifeAreas, noArea: { openTaskCount } }, per-area openTaskCount, taskCount and seriesCount, create and rename answering { lifeArea }, filter value none, delete answering 204. The tests import the type LifeAreasResponse from src/shared/api (plan Task 2); tsc -b over test/ fails until plan Tasks 1 and 2 land, as test/series.test.ts did for SeriesDto.

Assertions that go beyond the literal PRD text, each taken from the plan or from a risk it names, so R-IMPL-LEAK can judge them:
- a decomposed accent (u plus combining acute) equals the precomposed one in lifeAreaNameKey and is a 409 duplicate on create (plan Risks row, NFC step);
- PATCH /api/life-areas/:id refuses id, description and a typo with 400 naming the key, and an empty body with 400 (plan Task 5 closed allowlist; PRD What We're NOT Building keeps description unexposed);
- an ended series with no occurrence rows still counts in its area's seriesCount (PRD AC-6 "its Recurrence Series"; an ended series loses the area on delete too);
- an empty-string or non-string lifeAreaId is 400 naming lifeAreaId on all four write paths (plan Task 4 helper: only absent or null means no area);
- ADR-0009 guard: the 400, 404 and 409 messages of the new route and of the unknown-area refusals match no English marker word; only the field name is pinned, never a whole sentence;
- GET /api/tasks without lifeAreaId lists area-bearing Tasks and serialises a Task with exactly the thirteen documented fields (PRD AC-11 "byte-identical"; the existing contract suite only holds area-less Tasks and could not tell a missing filter from one defaulting to none);
- lifeAreaId=none composes with limit as the first N of the ordered filtered set, and with priority=normal counting an unset priority as normal (the frozen read contract).

Not encoded as tests, by the PRD's own TDD routing and the methodology's scope: the documentation half of AC-11 (docs/api-reference.md amended) is verified by plan Task 10's VALIDATE checks, not a test file.

